  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193755/assets/45a75f709f4a1eb10ac610b25b38689339b00e1b8e08af54486517a1c3a9084d.png)\n\nThe diagram shows an equilateral triangle, a regular pentagon, and a regular octagon. Find the measure of the shaded angle.",
  "worked_solution": "Note, that the full angle is the sum of the measure of interior angles of an equilateral triangle, a regular octagon, a regular pentagon, and the measure of the shaded angle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193755/assets/1289ebe280e4fc07cc71a7f45a8660c6ce29fb367567f07e1efb4d3712cbe767.png)\n\n \n\nFor a regular polygon with $n$ sides, the  measure of each interior angle must be\n\n$$\n\\frac{{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)}{n}.\n$$\n\n \n\n In an equilateral triangle, there are $3$ equivalent interior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{180}^{∘}(3-2)}{3}={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a regular octagon, there are $8$ equivalent interior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{180}^{∘}(8-2)}{8}={135}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nA regular pentagon has $5$ equivalent interior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{180}^{∘}(5-2)}{5}={108}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet us call $x$ the measure of the shaded angle. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{60}^{∘}+{135}^{∘}+{108}^{∘}+x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {303}^{∘}+x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={57}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
